I demand that XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXXXXXX and the credit reporting agencies immediately correct this error, as the previous investigation failed to comply with the Fair Credit Reporting Act ( FCRA ). 

2. Failure to Conduct a Proper Investigation Violation of FCRA 1681i ( a ) ( 1 ) ( A ) Under 15 U.S.C. 1681i ( a ) ( 1 ) ( A ), when a consumer disputes a late payment, the credit reporting agencies and the data furnisher must conduct a full, reasonable, and thorough investigation to verify its accuracy.

Merely referring to an account statement is NOT a legally sufficient method of verifying a late payment. 
Federal law requires a comprehensive review of all account records, including payment processing logs, internal account notes, and system-generated timestamps. 
Failure to conduct such an investigation is a violation of the Fair Credit Reporting Act ( FCRA ). 

3. Account Statements Are NOT Sufficient Proof of a Late Payment Violation of FCRA Compliance Standards Credit furnishers can not simply rely on account statements or billing summaries to verify a late payment.

Regulatory guidance confirms that verifiable documentation, such as internal transaction records, payment timestamps, and payment processing logs, must be reviewed.

The burden of proof is on the furnisher and credit bureaus to ensure accurate reporting, and failure to do so constitutes willful noncompliance. 

4. Furnishing Inaccurate Information Violation of FCRA 1681s-2 ( a ) ( 1 ) ( A ) Credit furnishers are legally obligated to ensure the accuracy and integrity of reported data.

If XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXXXXXX failed to verify actual payment timestamps or transaction records, then their reporting is in violation of federal law. 

5. Violation of the Truth in Lending Act ( TILA ) 15 U.S.C. 1601 et seq.

TILA 1638 ( a ) requires financial institutions to provide consumers with clear and accurate billing statements and transaction histories.

If the reported late payment is inconsistent with actual payment records, XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXX XXXXXXXX is in violation of TILAs accuracy requirements. 

6. Violation of the California Consumer Credit Reporting Agencies Act ( CCRAA ) Cal. Civ. Code 1785.25 Knowingly furnishing false or inaccurate information to a credit bureau is illegal under California law. 
