s and inconsistencies ( e.g. incorrect personal data and account details ) across the three bureaus, indicating a failure to maintain reasonable procedures to assure accuracy. Failure to Investigate ( FCRA 611 ( a ) ( 1 ) ( A ) ) : Upon receiving a dispute from a consumer, credit bureaus must investigate the disputed information and respond within 30 days. I submitted disputes regarding the fraudulent accounts and inquiries on my reports, yet more than 30 days have passed without resolution or adequate response. This lack of prompt investigation violates the FCRAs dispute resolution timeline. Failure to Reinvestigate ( FCRA 611 ( a ) ( 6 ) ) : If a consumer notifies a credit bureau that a dispute has not been resolved satisfactorily, the bureau is obligated to reinvestigate. I communicated continued concerns and provided additional information indicating identity theft, but the bureaus failed to reinvestigate or provide the required notice of results, in violation of FCRA 611 ( a ) ( 6 ). Failure to Protect Personal Information ( Gramm-Leach-Bliley Act, 15 U.S.C. 68016809 ) : The Gramm-Leach-Bliley Act requires financial institutions ( including credit bureaus ) to safeguard consumers personal data. The occurrence of this identity theft and the unauthorized access/opening of accounts in my name suggest that my personal information was not adequately protected, a breach of the data security and privacy obligations under GLBA. Identity Theft Protections Ignored ( Identity Theft and Assumption Deterrence Act, 18 U.S.C. 1028 ) : Identity theft is a federal crime under the Identity Theft and Assumption Deterrence Act. The fraudulent accounts on my credit reports demonstrate that an identity theft incident occurred. Equifax, Experian, and TransUnion failed to prevent or promptly address the fraudulent use of my identity, undermining the protections intended by this law and related identity theft regulations. Lack of Support for Identity Theft Victim ( FCRA, 15 U.S.C. 1681