12566906,2025-03-19,Credit reporting or other personal consumer reports,Credit reporting,Improper use of your report,Credit inquiries on your report that you don't recognize,"I am filing this complaint against TransUnion for failing to remove multiple unauthorized hard inquiries from my credit report, despite my formal disputes and supporting documentation. On XX/XX/year>, I visited a car dealership to apply for auto financing. My credit was subsequently pulled multiple times by different financial institutions without my explicit authorization. 

Legal Violations and Basis for Immediate Removal Under the Fair Credit Reporting Act ( FCRA, 15 U.S.C. 1681b ), a hard inquiry is only permissible when the consumer has provided explicit authorization to the entity conducting the inquiry and the inquiry is conducted for a legitimate credit transaction in which the consumer is involved. In my case, I never provided individual authorization for these inquiries, nor did I establish any credit relationship with most of these lenders.

Furthermore, under FCRA 611 ( 15 U.S.C. 1681i ), any inaccurate, unverified, or unauthorized information must be removed immediately from a consumers credit report. Additionally, under FCRA 1681c-2, when a credit inquiry is related to fraud or was conducted without the consumers authorization, the credit bureau must remove it within 4 business days of receiving the request and the necessary documentation.

Despite submitting a formal dispute to TransUnion, requesting the removal of these inquiries, they have failed to take appropriate action within the legally required timeframe.

Supporting Documentation Provided to TransUnion I submitted a copy of my FTC Identity Theft Report ( # XXXX ), confirming that these inquiries were made without my consent, a copy of my credit report with the disputed inquiries highlighted, and a copy of my government-issued ID verifying my identity. 

